{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"agent_message","text":"OK so fused projection gives about 5% geomean improvement. Not huge, but meaningful. Let me think about other optimizations.\n\nLet me look deeper into the biggest remaining cost centers. The profile showed softmax is 15-40%. Let me think about what else we can do.\n\nKey insight: **p0 and p1 share the same logits matrix** (just symmetrized for p1). Can we compute things more efficiently?\n\nAlso, I notice the `alpha == 1.0` path is separate but there's only 1 case where alpha=1.0 out of 6.\n\nLet me think about the norm computation more carefully. The Gram-matrix approach computes `p @ G` where G is (B, L, L) and p is (B, H, L, L). This is O(B * H * L^3) if done naively — wait, no, matmul on last two dims with broadcasting: p[b,h] @ G[b] is (L, L) @ (L, L) = L^3 multiply-adds, done B*H times. Total: B * H * L^3.\n\nFor B1L256H20: 1 * 20 * 256^3 = 335M ops. That's a lot.\nWait but the Gram approach was faster than direct `p @ x` for the large C case... let me recheck.\n\nDirect: `p @ x.unsqueeze(1)` is (B,H,L,L) @ (B,1,L,C) -> (B,H,L,C). That's B*H*L*L*C = 1*20*256*256*1280 = 1.68B ops. So Gram (335M) is indeed better when C > L.\n\nBut wait — for the norms we need `||p@x|| = sqrt(diag(p @ G @ p^T))`. Is there a way to compute just the diagonal more efficiently?\n\nActually `(pG * p).sum(dim=-1)` = row-wise dot product of pG and p = diagonal of pG @ p^T. The `pG = p @ G` costs B*H*L^3. Then the sum is B*H*L^2.\n\nAlternative: compute `x^T @ p^T` first? No, that's the same cost.\n\nActually wait — I can compute the norm differently. Since `||u||^2 = sum_c u_c^2` and `u = p @ x`:\n`u_c = sum_k p_k x_{k,c}`\n`||u||^2 = sum_c (sum_k p_k x_{k,c})^2`\n= `sum_c sum_k sum_j p_k p_j x_{k,c} x_{j,c}`\n= `sum_k sum_j p_k p_j (sum_c x_{k,c} x_{j,c})`\n= `sum_k sum_j p_k p_j G_{k,j}`\n\nThat's exactly what we're computing. No way around the cost.\n\nBut wait — maybe we can compute both norm1 and normm from the same p1 @ G product? \n\n`||m||^2 = ||p_mix @ x||^2 = diag(p_mix @ G @ p_mix^T)`\n`p_mix = (1-beta) * p0 + beta * p1`\n\n`||m||^2 = (1-beta)^2 * ||p0@x||^2 + beta^2 * ||p1@x||^2 + 2*(1-beta)*beta * <p0@x, p1@x>_rowwise`\n\nThe cross term is `(p0 @ G) * p1` summed over last dim. So we need p0@G too. We still need 2 GEMMs (p0@G and p1@G). And we need p0 anyway for... wait, do we have p0 already? Yes!\n\nBut we currently compute p_mix @ G instead of p0 @ G. Let's see:\n- Current: p1 @ G and p_mix @ G → 2 matmuls\n- Alternative: p0 @ G and p1 @ G → 2 matmuls (same cost)\n\nSo no difference. Unless we can compute both more efficiently.\n\nActually, let me think about another angle. What if we compute `u1 = p1 @ x` directly for all cases where C is small enough that it's faster than Gram? Then we get both the norm and can use it for... no, wait, we don't need u1 itself. We only need the norm of u1 and m.\n\nHmm, let me explore another direction: **can we reduce the softmax cost?**\n\nActually, let me try a completely different approach to the Gram matrix. Instead of computing G = x @ x^T which is B*L^2*C, what if we think about it differently?\n\nWait, here's an idea. We have:\n- `||u1||` = norm of `p1 @ x` per row \n- We could compute `u1 = p1 @ x` then take norms\n\nFor the B1L256C1280H20 case: p1@x costs 20 * 256 * 256 * 1280 = 1.68B ops (direct) vs 20 * 256^3 = 335M ops (Gram). Gram is clearly better.\n\nBut for B1L64C512H8: 8 * 64 * 64 * 512 = 16.8M (direct) vs 8 * 64^3 = 2M (Gram). Still Gram better.\n\nActually Gram is always better when C > L, which is true for all cases except maybe none. Let's check: L=64, C=512 → C>L. L=256, C=640 → C>L. Always true.\n\nOK, let me try a different idea. What if we avoid computing the full symmetric logits matrix by using a different softmax formulation?\n\nActually, let me think about what's really slow. In the profile, for the B1L256C640H10 case:\n- sym+p1 softmax: 40% of total\n- norms+ratio: 27.6%\n- q+k+v proj: 33.4%\n\nThe softmax cost is high relative to the logits matmul cost. This is because softmax is memory-bound while GEMM is compute-bound, and with single thread, memory bandwidth matters a lot.\n\nLet me try an optimization: instead of `a_p * logits + a_m * logits.transpose(-1,-2)`, which creates a new tensor, do it fully in-place. Also, compute softmax on contiguous data.\n\nWait, I already tested that and it showed improvement. Let me try incorporating the best ideas carefully.\n\nActually let me go deeper — what if we can avoid computing p1 softmax entirely? Can we compute the norm of the symmetric attention output without explicitly computing p1?\n\nNo, the norm depends on softmax of the symmetrized logits, which is a different distribution than p0.\n\nLet me try another angle: **torch.compile or torch.jit?** No, those are probably not allowed (they'd count as persistent state or runtime reconfiguration).\n\nLet me try the actual best ideas from my experiments and run the actual evaluation. The fused projection plus in-place operations seem like the most reliable wins. Let me also try the `bmm` approach for the logits computation to see if it's more efficient.\n\nLet me try a more carefully tuned version and run the actual evaluation.\n<br>Let me now implement the best optimized version — fusing the q/k/v projections into one GEMM plus in-place operations for the symmetric logits — and run the full Dev evaluation.\n<br>\n<br>Let me write the optimized method to `method.py`:"}}
